md/pci -- PCIe root complex, e1000 + cmd646 IDE
===============================================
An e1000 NIC (bound by the Ophir FCode in src/obp) and a cmd646 IDE controller;
no Legion disk.

    make md  TARGET=pci
    make all TARGET=pci

Needs a QEMU with vpci and cmd646 support (not in upstream yet) and patches/
(applied at build time).

QEMU command line
-----------------
    qemu-system-sparc64 -M niagara -accel tcg,thread=single -L <outdir> \
        -m 4096 -serial mon:stdio -nographic \
        -netdev user,id=net0 \
        -device e1000,netdev=net0,addr=0x0,mac=52:54:00:66:00:20,romfile= \
        -device cmd646-ide,id=ide0,addr=0x2 \
        -drive if=none,id=disk0,file=<disk.img>,format=raw \
        -device ide-hd,drive=disk0,bus=ide0.0,unit=0

-accel tcg,thread=single matters for long workloads such as an OS install:
without it multi-hour stalls were measured (lock contention in QEMU's
multi-threaded TCG).

    net    /pci@0/network@0         e1000 at PCI device 0
    disk   /pci@0/ide@2/disk@0      cmd646 IDE at device 2, primary master
    cdrom  /pci@0/ide@2/cdrom@1,0   primary slave

Tested by hand (no gate boots pci): Solaris 10 u11 on cmd646 boots to the login
prompt, root logs in, e1000g1 comes up, 200 MB written and read on the disk
without an error. OpenSolaris b134 (the install image on the cmd646 CD drive)
reaches "Enter user name for system maintenance". See build_matrix, section 8.

What is in the guest MD (md/pci/1up.pdesc)
------------------------------------------
Everything not listed here is as in md/legion/1up.pdesc (common.pdesc, cpu,
memory, platform). The pci machine differs from legion in these places. Where a
value comes from is also in md/REFERENCE, part 2.

The iodevice node pci_bus (under phys_io)
    Most property lines are those of T2-archive:t1_fpga/src/xilinx/configs/
    1c1t_guest.desc, which describes the same hardware (device-type "pciex").
    The values are set by code: md/REFERENCE part 2 names the readers, "The
    iodevice values" at its end what sets each.
    Not taken from that file: name, pseudonym, ino-bitmap (nothing reads them,
    md/REFERENCE note 38).
    Different here (ours):
        probe-list           "0,1,2" (e1000 at 0, spare slot 1, IDE at 2);
                             there "0"
        interrupt-map-mask   keeps all five device-number bits (0xfff8)
        bus-ranges           starts at bus 0 (there 2: a PCIe switch sits in
                             front)

Ours, with the example of 1c1t_guest.desc
    The interrupt-map-entry nodes, one per slot.
    child-interrupt 1, parent-interrupt 0x14..0x16 and parent-device-path
    /pci@0 are the values of the example; it has four entries (INTA..INTD of
    one device), we have three (INTA of three devices) with other
    child-unit-address values.
    Node format: FWARC 2007/386.

Must agree with the hypervisor MD
    pci_bus.cfg-handle 0 = pcie_bus.cfghandle of md/pci/1up.hdesc.
    OBP names the node /pci@<cfg-handle>, so this 0 is the "pci@0" of the
    device paths.

Ours only
    aliases net, disk, disk0, cdrom and boot-device "disk disk0 cdrom"
        the device paths of the QEMU command line above
    platform mac-address 0x525400660020
        equal to the MAC of -device e1000,mac=, so the banner and the
        interface agree
    no pci-network node: the Ophir FCode reads the MAC from the NIC (see the
        comment in 1up.pdesc)

Removed from legion
    the virtual-device disk node (and its fwd arc), the alias vdisk,
    boot-device "disk"
